<figure class="pairing-diagram reckoning-visual">
  <header>
    <p>Why parity decides the route</p>
    <h3>Arrivals and departures consume edges in pairs</h3>
  </header>

  <div class="pairing-shell">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 900 420" role="img" aria-labelledby="pairing-title pairing-desc">
      <title id="pairing-title">Five edges meeting one vertex, grouped into two enter-leave pairs with one edge left over</title>
      <desc id="pairing-desc">
        A central vertex has degree five. Four incident edges form two striped pairs, while a fifth magenta edge remains unpaired and must be used to start or end a trail.
      </desc>
      <defs>
        <pattern id="pair-hatch" width="10" height="10" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse" patternTransform="rotate(45)">
          <line x1="0" x2="0" y2="10" />
        </pattern>
      </defs>

      <g class="edge-bundle">
        <path class="paired one" d="M450 210L160 76" />
        <path class="paired one" d="M450 210L116 184" />
        <path class="paired two" d="M450 210L165 342" />
        <path class="paired two" d="M450 210L558 382" />
        <path class="unpaired" d="M450 210L790 116" />
      </g>

      <path class="pair-loop one" d="M151 62C67 87 54 173 105 203C160 235 228 179 209 112" />
      <path class="pair-fill" d="M151 62C67 87 54 173 105 203C160 235 228 179 209 112" fill="url(#pair-hatch)" />
      <path class="pair-loop two" d="M151 353C194 414 435 430 570 390" />

      <circle class="central-vertex" cx="450" cy="210" r="42" />
      <text class="degree" x="450" y="204" text-anchor="middle">degree</text>
      <text class="degree-number" x="450" y="234" text-anchor="middle">5</text>

      <text class="pair-label one" x="72" y="48">enter + leave</text>
      <text class="pair-label two" x="282" y="397">enter + leave</text>
      <text class="leftover-label" x="790" y="91" text-anchor="end">one edge left over</text>
      <text class="endpoint-label" x="790" y="143" text-anchor="end">start or finish here</text>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> every intermediate visit uses one edge to enter and one to leave.
    An odd-degree vertex has one unpaired edge, so an open Euler trail can accommodate only two of
    them: one at the start and one at the finish.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

